The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} relies on an inductive argument on the number of layers, combining Cram\'er’s theorem for empirical means of i.i.d.\ random variables with a careful analysis of the continuity properties of the neural network architecture, in a structure somewhat analogous to the functional CLT for MLPs. Concretely:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Next we establish exponential tightness of the sequence $(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} f^{(L)}_{(n)}(\cdot))_{n\geq 1}$ on $C(\mathcal{X},\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}})$, endowed with the uniform topology, using a variant of Schied’s criterion tailored to the layer-by-layer propagation of randomness and taking into account the sub-linear growth of activation functions.
\item[(ii)] We then first prove that for a fixed finite set of inputs, the LDP for the corresponding finite-dimensional network outputs holds, relying on Chaganty’s lemma, combined with exponential equivalence arguments, and Cram\'er’s theorem under local exponential moment assumptions, and crucially using epi-convergence techniques from convex analysis to obtain joint lower semi-continuity.
\item[(iii)] To conclude, we lift the finite-input LDPs to an LDP for the entire process by means of a standard application of the Dawson--G\"artner theorem and the inverse contraction principle.
\end{enumerate}

