expanded_sketch:
The proof “partially follows the lines in Bellettini–Mantegazza–Novaga, *Motion by curvature of planar networks* (2004)”, with the novelty of “accounting for boundary terms and for the tangential component of the velocity, that we denote with \( \lambda_\eps:=\langle \partial_t \gamma_\eps, \tau_{\gamma_\eps}\rangle \)”.

The “crucial point is to obtain $\varepsilon$-independent integral estimates of the curvature and all its derivatives.” Concretely, “by an ODE's argument,” since for $0<\varepsilon\le 1$ “all the flows … start from a common initial smooth curve,” then for any fixed $j\in\mathbb N$ there is “a common positive interval of time such that all the quantities $\|\partial^i_s \kappa\|_{L^2}$, for $i\in\{0,\dots,j\}$ are equibounded.”

These uniform bounds “allow us to get compactness and $C^\infty$ convergence to the curvature flow as $\varepsilon\to 0^+$.” In establishing the main theorem, the convergence is asserted to hold “up to $\Tsingmcf$, and not only up to $\Tmax$,” where $\Tmax$ is the time given by the following uniform estimate:

\begin{Proposition}\label{prop:uniform_estimate_of_k_square}
There exists $\Tmax >0$ such that, for any $T \in (0, \Tmax)$ 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:uniform_estimate_of_k_square}
\sup_{\eps\in(0,1]}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}
\int_0^{\ell(\gaet)} \kgaet^2~ds < +\infty.
\end{equation}
\end{Proposition}

The discussion also explains why the Dirichlet case is manageable: one can use “the fact that the velocity \( \partial_t \gamma \) vanishes at the boundary,” so one can “discard all boundary terms arising from integration by parts,” whereas for other boundary conditions the tangential velocity at the boundary produces contributions that are “difficult to estimate.”

