expanded_sketch:
Using a standard subordination argument for fractional powers of generators of Markov processes, the fractional heat content admits the probabilistic representation
\[
Q^{(\alpha)}_\Omega(t)=\int_{\Omega}\P_x\left(\mbox{$B^\alpha(s)\in \Omega$ for all $0\leqslant s\leqslant t$} \right) dx,
\]
where $B^\alpha$ is the subordinated horizontal Brownian motion on $\mathbb{G}$. The paper then proposes a “simplified argument based on Taylor formula and some refined estimates of exit probabilities of horizontal Brownian motion on Carnot groups,” yielding the small-time asymptotics for all $1\leqslant \alpha\leqslant 2$.

The main idea is to pass to a functional version: for any nonnegative measurable $f$ on $\mathbb{G}$,
\begin{align}\label{eq:Q_f}
 		Q^{(\alpha)}_f(t)=\int_{\mathbb G}\E_x\left[\inf_{0\le s\le t} f(B^\alpha(s))\right]dx.
 	\end{align}
When $f=\mathbbm{1}_\Omega$, this equals the fractional heat content. “Using the Taylor formula on Carnot groups” (Bonfiglioli), the authors “prove the asymptotics of the displayed identity \eqref{eq:Q_f} for compactly supported smooth functions $f$,” and then “the lower bound of asymptotic heat content follows by an approximation argument.” We first record the lower bound precisely:

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:liminf}
 		Let $\Omega$ be any bounded, open subset of $\mathbb{G}$ having finite horizontal perimeter. Then,
 		\begin{align*}
 			\liminf_{t\to 0}\frac{|\Omega|-Q^{(\alpha)}_\Omega(t)}{\mu_\alpha(t)}\ge |\partial\Omega|_{H}.
 		\end{align*}
 	\end{proposition}

To obtain the matching upper bound (and hence the full limit in establishing the main theorem), boundary regularity is used: “The regularity conditions on the boundary are needed for proving the upper bound of the limit,” and the approach “mainly requires some estimates of the probabilities of small exit times.” We first prove the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}\label{lem:estimate1}
 	There exist constants $c,\beta>0$ such that for all $R>0$,
 	\begin{equation}\label{eq:exit_probability}
 		\begin{aligned}
 			\P_0\left(\sup_{0\le s\le t}d(B(s),0)> R\right)&\le e^{-\frac{R^2}{ct}}+\sum_{p=1}^k\mathcal{G}_p(R,t)+\sum_{p=1}^k\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_p\left(R,t\right),
 		\end{aligned}
 	\end{equation}
 		where 
 		\begin{equation}\label{eq:G_g}
 			\begin{aligned}
 				\mathcal{G}_p(R,t)&=\exp\left(-\frac{R^{p\beta}}{ct^{\frac{\beta(p+1)}{2}+1}}\right)+\exp\left(-\frac{R^{p\beta}}{ct^{\frac{p\beta}{2}+1}}\right), \\
 				\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_p(R,t)&=\exp\left(-\frac{R^{\beta(p+1)+2}}{ct^{\frac{\beta p}{2}+\beta+2}}\right)+\exp\left(-\frac{R^{\beta p+2}}{ct^{\frac{\beta p}{2}+2}}\right) \\
 				&+\exp\left(-\frac{R^{\beta(p+1)+2}}{ct^{\frac{\beta(p+1)^2+4p+2}{2p}}}\right)+\exp\left(-\frac{R^{p\beta+2}}{ct^{\frac{p\beta}{2}+2+\frac{1}{p}}}\right).
 			\end{aligned}
 		\end{equation}
 	\end{theorem}

These exit-probability estimates, together with a Taylor type expansion of smooth functions on Carnot groups, yield the needed upper bound. The “assumption of non-characteristic boundary is very crucial,” since the method requires “$C^2$ regularity of the signed distance function from the boundary of the domain.”

